#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524033 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "071c8f49-d804-5f98-8a95-def2b9471668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523273} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf84118-2059-5f36-ba3c-60ecf7e48225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523382} {:answer/id #uuid "94a42c42-afe5-541a-ba5c-8988133fd11f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523567} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a87f55-8422-523f-8f58-b46dfbaf6a1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523641} {:answer/id #uuid "dec1957e-5a0e-5556-92fc-2e137c12ed35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523721}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523382} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1873516-1ea7-5025-ba34-62e81accf8f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f06b7511-18eb-5f50-9aea-3636371eb6fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143658" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{e}^{y}, & -1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>2,\\,y>\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>2,\\,y>\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/19/19510c5eb927edbc93ff077f13e645820548e9074da0e1101d9ef641650642bb.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=D.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo our region $Δ$ covers the entire region where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{D}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a10c141-494b-5ffe-b789-4d235572d460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524600} {:answer/id #uuid "94c5f700-7024-5ad9-9fb8-06de616c6958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524619} {:answer/id #uuid "a350e58a-9fe9-5a48-a78c-f6ab313c004a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524643} {:answer/id #uuid "f085a1c6-cfcd-5341-bcfa-3848ba092d59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524805} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb5fdb8-bd3a-507b-bfd0-0f58c8d15589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524835}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524619} :answer-field/id #uuid "54963578-cc23-5bef-b243-20d224d918a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "02ed11c8-435c-5d94-883f-47ba8fa464eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143377" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{2}{3}y$ for $y\\in [1,2]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}y{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [1,2].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}(2-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [1,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y}_{f(x,y)}=\\underbrace{(4{x}^{3})}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot \\underbrace{(\\frac{2}{3}y)}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bda0597-d4eb-501c-8f78-9cc0d69850ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524460} {:answer/id #uuid "53da8f36-10c8-5cc6-86e4-472caa08a221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524526} {:answer/id #uuid "5609f0e7-a55f-54bb-b60f-a7f0957c755b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524531} {:answer/id #uuid "9816d79b-cb35-5e33-8ecc-f8d36301c91e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524624} {:answer/id #uuid "dfba38fe-2d5e-512c-9841-9b766813abf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524526} :answer-field/id #uuid "56c85a06-4467-5818-826d-ae5c1ae05ad9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "04a40517-0aa4-5398-b54b-804112b670f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-96739" :question/problem "The distance covered per game by a particular soccer player is uniformly distributed between $10\\,\\text{km}$\n and $13\\,\\text{km}$ inclusive. What is the probability that this player runs $12\\,\\text{km}$ or more in a randomly selected game?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the distance covered by the player. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[10,13],$ and we wish to compute $P(X\\ge 12).$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a}, & a\\le x\\le b \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[10,13],$ we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}, & 10\\le x\\le 13 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 12) & ={\\int }_{12}^{13}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{12}^{13}\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{x}{3}|}_{12}^{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{12}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "310a997c-e1ee-509f-b5a5-c83f303016e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524471} {:answer/id #uuid "96855423-2c14-53c8-9841-2610cea6223a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524621} {:answer/id #uuid "afa9fea7-5a60-5e00-be15-b60751c4ec57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524665} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d3546d-16f4-553d-bc58-12b528100c36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524779} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b9033f-aefa-5a72-bc8f-61f04664c8aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524806}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524665} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e41b8cd-f547-56b5-b624-9d4998a39501" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "09b415e6-d89a-5c4c-91d7-faeef6a95b62" :question/math-academy-id "q-143387" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}(x+y),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{1}{4}(y+\\frac{3}{2})$ for $y\\in [2,3]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+yx]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2+2y-\\frac{1}{2}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(y+\\frac{3}{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [2,3].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=2}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+\\frac{9}{2}-2x-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+\\frac{5}{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [1,2].$ So, when $x\\in [1,2]$ and $y\\in [2,3],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{4}(x+y)}_{f(x,y)}\\ne \\underbrace{\\frac{1}{4}(x+\\frac{5}{2})}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\frac{1}{4}(y+\\frac{3}{2})}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is *not* true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22b3a3fc-340b-5ed5-8d5e-990986db2466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524444} {:answer/id #uuid "411a9cec-3a8e-59f5-9ad3-e086842f889a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524502} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca1210b-5ee2-5865-924d-eb975f41a198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524559} {:answer/id #uuid "8f6c7f06-285b-5716-8654-ada585b5cad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{16{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524608} {:answer/id #uuid "e274eb34-e730-5088-9d44-3c0f08b82dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524608} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b00f6e3-8cc3-5080-b638-0c0a437c40d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1706fef5-2c32-54fe-bdcf-eb9228266f86" :question/math-academy-id "q-143364" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}, & \\,0\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}y, & \\,3\\le y\\le 5, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\ge 2,3\\le Y\\le 4).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}y{e}^{-x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,\\infty )\\times [3,5].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}y{e}^{-x}, & \\,0\\le x,\\,3\\le y\\le 5 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2,3\\le Y\\le 4) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{8}y{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{-x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{3}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{16}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{b}\\frac{7}{16}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{7{e}^{-x}}{16}]}_{2}^{b} \\\\  & =\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{7{e}^{-b}}{16}+\\frac{7{e}^{-2}}{16}] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{16{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524966 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "075ff70c-8adb-53e9-8868-dac30ec95b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524395} {:answer/id #uuid "11f6e6ae-3c6c-50c6-99cc-458271767c9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524412} {:answer/id #uuid "a69c5953-f455-53cf-a745-4db7c010554c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524646} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf1af4e-c02d-5acb-a898-ed1acac8e812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524755}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524755} :answer-field/id #uuid "65f9587c-2102-51c3-9018-cf73e669ee77" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524882} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e2b8556-b100-5f8e-bc9f-9cd87c21cb7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524513} {:answer/id #uuid "72385776-67d2-5118-b12a-b9ffbb9a1976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{18\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524565} {:answer/id #uuid "92909ea3-1683-5e8f-b3a3-8b2af56ef7af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524612} {:answer/id #uuid "e88ad1ac-4369-5a83-8114-9aecaa890952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524612} :answer-field/id #uuid "b41532f6-c5f8-55db-9dde-0e3b62dd91b0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1741c2e1-8000-5ded-a8b9-94a357d2c64a" :question/math-academy-id "q-343887" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right))+x(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation $$\n\\displaystyle {y}^{″}+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right):\n$$\n\n$$\n[-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\pi A=0 \\\\ 3\\pi B=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{27\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "344d0e42-cd70-5ad8-90fc-7db764816086" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524478} {:answer/id #uuid "86854eda-5a8f-50ea-94b0-dd55b64266ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524590} {:answer/id #uuid "ce713a1f-b2ec-53d9-97c8-17515419704d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524724} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1a145b-0c0c-558c-963e-71228c0de2a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524800}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524590} :answer-field/id #uuid "04a2bae6-aeac-5a01-8bbe-2da7affb6702" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524842} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e74e00-5bf0-5bcb-9c73-5ac12785eb89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{20(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+40)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524390} {:answer/id #uuid "20391762-3990-5977-9275-05105acc9764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+40)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524440} {:answer/id #uuid "96006c5e-26f2-501a-82c3-1c733fab3f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+40)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524620} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2ce446-0043-5cc6-9d9f-038477aa3b6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+40)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524662}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524662} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b8556e9-1a54-5d0b-a5a5-5efa77eeb553" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "234b7e73-d01a-5fb8-b7f2-7b7733987672" :question/math-academy-id "q-343886" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-10y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼10+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)-10(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))=10+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-10\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-10)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)=10+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-10\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=10 \\\\ {b}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-10)=\\frac{5(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-1 \\\\ {b}_{n}=\\frac{20(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+40)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{20(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+40)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93a95dfd-61c1-5b85-ad7a-7edfc0702959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524618}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524618} :answer-field/id #uuid "79cdb461-38af-5784-a292-e83653dfa433" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6138bd37-a9a0-5af4-80eb-ad7aa981c99c" :question/math-academy-id "q-341815" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & x\\in [0,3) \\\\ -4, & x\\in [-3,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{2}.$\n\n${a}_{2}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3.$ Also, $f(x)=1$ for $x\\in [0,3)$ and $f(x)=-4$ for $x\\in [-3,0).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}(-4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{0}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}x)]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi ))+\\frac{1}{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524995 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f89f754-b00e-5b7c-b63f-401fe4f471d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524409} {:answer/id #uuid "694234a6-597f-5e61-b5bf-84fc5b6aa6e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524554} {:answer/id #uuid "6c3f9669-23d7-5243-84e9-d09de434a07d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524557} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c0d009-d03a-5fb2-a0bd-6539af2c96d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524651} {:answer/id #uuid "ba99982b-5521-5081-812d-09016da48ec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524679}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524679} :answer-field/id #uuid "11562c89-3fe2-5b73-bf46-55642d35bb9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "75c2a299-1223-5668-b631-6cae0200f484" :question/math-academy-id "q-170687" :question/problem "In an online poker tournament, the maximum loss is $10$ dollars, and the maximum win is $8$ dollars. Assuming that a player's earnings are uniformly distributed between $-10$ and $8,$ what is the probability that a randomly chosen player loses between $2$ and $8$ dollars?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the earnings of a randomly chosen player. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[-10,8],$ and we wish to compute $P(-8\\le X\\le -2).$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a}, & a\\le x\\le b \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[-10,8],$ we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18}, & -10\\le x\\le 8 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-8\\le X\\le -2) & ={\\int }_{-8}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-8}^{-2}\\frac{1}{18}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{x}{18}|}_{-8}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{18}-\\left(-\\frac{8}{18}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e4cc218-73fe-5998-8ccf-bc16f3b90e09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+20)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524514} {:answer/id #uuid "b9355ba0-889c-5450-99e3-818ab0a4f2f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+20)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524676} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a1ae82-6bda-5f83-b1fc-96118fbd6f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+20)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524728} {:answer/id #uuid "e570c1cd-6353-5178-af72-bcd3aab8e4d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+20)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524783}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524514} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f9455a9-07ca-5997-b4ca-16fa2b6ab07c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524903} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35c42206-68dd-51fc-99b6-ae210d259dac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524482} {:answer/id #uuid "3b2f4c1a-00a1-55c0-9e44-6bcc54f3f7de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524488} {:answer/id #uuid "8c0a8364-45bf-574e-bb9d-0ac0a859448d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524603} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0bdb29-be00-5a7a-ae5b-f5917ddd3a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524725}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524482} :answer-field/id #uuid "e37207bf-0563-5832-ad30-c6a23c3c930c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9b306e59-ac03-5a23-8563-3ae450882f03" :question/math-academy-id "q-343882" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)-5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))=5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-5\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-5)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)=5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=5 \\\\ {b}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-5)=\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-1 \\\\ {b}_{n}=-\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+20)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+20)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525018 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51db8527-e691-524a-8d64-0cecd1ce2030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524523} {:answer/id #uuid "7442854e-4eef-58d2-937e-b8a32847ad0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524569} {:answer/id #uuid "7664a506-7a27-5659-88f7-2a7981b1344c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524571} {:answer/id #uuid "891c8039-b5b1-5884-8dca-6e836ef55867" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{140}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524598} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c5dd60-da56-5120-ac2a-e485b14f6897" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524741}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524741} :answer-field/id #uuid "91eb2137-924b-5cda-97be-891267d62252" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9d9e7f8d-7ad5-5408-a4ca-eaab80943f96" :question/math-academy-id "q-96870" :question/problem "The minimum time it takes an electrician to fix a power outage is $20$ minutes, and the maximum time is $160$ minutes. Assuming that the time it takes to fix the power outage is uniformly distributed, what is the probability that a random power outage fix takes more than $30$ minutes but less than $60$ minutes?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the time it takes to fix a random power outage. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[20,160],$ and we wish to compute $P(30<X<60).$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a}, & a\\le x\\le b \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[20,160],$ we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{140}, & 20\\le x\\le 160 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(30<X<60) & ={\\int }_{30}^{60}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{30}^{60}\\frac{1}{140}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{x}{140}|}_{30}^{60} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{140}-\\frac{30}{140} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00062ecf-0f51-5e19-9310-d192161a5246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524377} {:answer/id #uuid "552517bf-8975-5614-bc42-fbfb2782e608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524528} {:answer/id #uuid "be80291f-0de3-5c39-a291-04b336487ab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524686} {:answer/id #uuid "d80e92db-3cf5-5e65-b11c-1182c3250e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524744} {:answer/id #uuid "f6767626-734c-5b0e-a601-dd3d629fe5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524744} :answer-field/id #uuid "e761c330-86b4-51d2-b2a4-8c7d84a3cd9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e042f50a-1181-570f-8ece-0feac63fb8cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-96859" :question/problem "A computer program is designed so that it can randomly generate a real number between $0$ and $10,$ inclusive. What is the probability that the number generated is between $3$ and $7?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number generated by the program. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[0,10],$ and we wish to compute $P(3<X<7).$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a}, & a\\le x\\le b \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[0,10],$ we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}, & 0\\le x\\le 10 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<X<7) & ={\\int }_{3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{7}\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{x}{10}|}_{3}^{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}-\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525056 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1250b344-6a3f-5e22-8259-6b6f4e1fd664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524415} {:answer/id #uuid "3b42918c-1af0-5846-b7eb-f58a3c71f7d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524490} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fdf54d-8e0d-564f-bace-2883c73dc5d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524667} {:answer/id #uuid "d1883d56-92de-539c-8f2f-861ea03c9e84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524729} {:answer/id #uuid "f040bfd2-f972-53d7-af55-e0a4c410abc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524804}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524415} :answer-field/id #uuid "cca69172-8d2b-50ee-94f0-4248d1848b28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e55c9ea3-689a-5b3d-8e62-79deec4589d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-96738" :question/problem "The duration of a tv program is uniformly distributed between $52$ and $66$ minutes, inclusive. What is the probability that a randomly chosen program lasts $60$ minutes or less?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the duration of the tv program. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[52,66],$ and we wish to compute $P(X\\le 60).$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a}, & a\\le x\\le b \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[52,66],$ we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{14}, & 52\\le x\\le 66 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 60) & ={\\int }_{52}^{60}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{52}^{60}\\frac{1}{14}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{x}{14}|}_{52}^{60} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{14}-\\frac{52}{14} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ff6638-43e0-5544-89de-9608b0711546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524397} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6a586f-0cf2-5116-a740-1a9d8f1242c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524508} {:answer/id #uuid "88a6fa17-dfc1-5da9-b77a-35d12299dbb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524596} {:answer/id #uuid "c944dd89-80c1-5096-989e-9c5466682fc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524709} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2ca45a-40c5-5ab4-aa0e-968f3478e0f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524722}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524709} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d768191-43c2-52ef-8bd4-abb5c4c7e134" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fa1fc563-4426-5908-b1bd-3188967331c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-332719" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1cd11c63564050d5851c53e6a808e6a7b73ee33ce6cbe77c9278c6a3429eaec5.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac8b5d3-4035-5dd3-a5dd-ebf6500b19f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526526} {:answer/id #uuid "1319139a-d5dc-5725-94ac-d3880fa8552c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526540} {:answer/id #uuid "39126990-5fa4-5f41-bc2f-d291064aff94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526610} {:answer/id #uuid "94939d5f-6f1b-5dd9-ac7a-3a24e4324e85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526793} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe28192-dd92-5dbb-b0a6-33f806a9c962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526811}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526811} :answer-field/id #uuid "021bcd34-e94e-5e91-97f6-6e6c378cff60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "02209bdf-5c25-5d71-9f0c-90055349806b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159335" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{xy-y}{x}, & x\\ge 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & x\\ge 1,\\,y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal CDF of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{xy-y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{y(x-1)}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =y\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =y\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(1-0) \\\\  & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "717d95b7-fa98-5ce7-87da-3b5f14244fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}x{y}^{1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526722} {:answer/id #uuid "85f0574f-2eb3-5a2e-a9b4-0e626bc0fe9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}{y}^{1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526771} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad206e5-9b08-5529-8514-60481a8f3392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{5/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526781} {:answer/id #uuid "a5562678-6941-5fda-a0d2-04f3395f2ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{3}{y}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526819} {:answer/id #uuid "da74a110-efe7-5877-a2c6-a01a07f76757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526924}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526722} :answer-field/id #uuid "135ebc58-1d65-54f4-8dbd-b35d2b714a66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2603d8b7-b055-59f9-a9de-a5bcf92cb12b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159330" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y>1 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1, & x>2,\\,y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}x{y}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}x{y}^{1/2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527146 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0048806c-59ec-5b54-9603-d1aea47812e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3159" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526496} {:answer/id #uuid "654e6669-1c8f-5b7c-9105-507f8fec4f7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3413" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526706} {:answer/id #uuid "7e5e4424-4f3f-57f4-b362-4a5859daccd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3212" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526751} {:answer/id #uuid "b98dc844-9500-5f8d-b857-bb196f769cd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3461" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526856} {:answer/id #uuid "e03cd40f-b9b7-5e51-8c05-cf33954b77fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3665" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526706} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e93d9e4-a205-5e33-a4db-5e5a5cd44ab1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2f85c49f-b8cd-5032-99b8-bc70560e429d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143507" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{75}$ be a random sample of size $n=75$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.75,$ calculate $P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =75,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }x\\cdot \\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2{b}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =1.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.75.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(1.5,\\frac{0.75}{75}) \\\\  & ∼N(1.5,0.01).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6) & =P(\\frac{1.5-1.5}{\\sqrt{0.01}}<Z<\\frac{1.6-1.5}{\\sqrt{0.01}}) \\\\  & =P(0<Z<1) \\\\  & =P(Z<1)-P(Z<0) \\\\  & =Φ(1)-Φ(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1)=0.8413,\n$$\n\nand by the symmetry of the normal distribution, $Φ(0)=0.5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6) & =Φ(1)-Φ(0) \\\\  & =0.8413-0.5 \\\\  & =0.3413.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f14ebf1-a6a5-5a1c-bceb-b94a30140ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}-2{e}^{1-y}+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526570} {:answer/id #uuid "88fdae8b-27cd-5d83-8742-cb8138b1a6f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-{e}^{1-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526776} {:answer/id #uuid "c717cb6b-8e7f-518f-9495-eec458502f12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526885} {:answer/id #uuid "e402e686-3ff6-5db1-a2cc-d914d442a9da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526951} {:answer/id #uuid "e95e6242-3b0b-565c-aa1a-1ac96cd9dddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-{e}^{1-y}+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526965} :answer-field/id #uuid "4808f553-7313-53e6-9c9a-a373717e5764" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3a66a770-559e-57c4-8545-74993f2211db" :question/math-academy-id "q-159320" :question/problem "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{1-y}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}})(1-{e}^{1-y}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-{e}^{1-y}+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-{e}^{1-y}+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca9b839-cb5a-5b4b-bc58-e09b93f3700e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526528} {:answer/id #uuid "18bd9257-411d-5063-94ed-ecd8343134ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526556} {:answer/id #uuid "3fcd8e4f-e442-55ec-acdd-e2642ab81430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526628} {:answer/id #uuid "a0127b97-00fd-5391-a92c-e6076c707eb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526813} {:answer/id #uuid "cdab7955-73b1-53f6-a3ff-c803c784a336" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}x(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526896}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526528} :answer-field/id #uuid "e29738f2-3cd0-58f4-93b4-014da2a887b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "433f0ce6-f1e1-52cc-bff5-350654ecc1ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-159327" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{48}xy(x+y), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y>4 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>2,\\,y>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{48}xy(x+y))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+x\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+2y\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}(2x+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{24}(x+y), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "096fe8eb-0f73-5dcd-9283-5609e6cf6c6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{x{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526521} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7b246b-a91d-5397-9b3e-492593bcf3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526572} {:answer/id #uuid "9ffd77dc-caa5-5a7b-9077-2effd6a99afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526812} {:answer/id #uuid "c936279a-3486-5ccb-b9e0-fffe1ccea34f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526891} {:answer/id #uuid "f30d0020-1a54-5639-ab8d-73a8529f890a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526984}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526812} :answer-field/id #uuid "20ab855f-f5a7-5070-a185-6c452a9551a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "74fc0efc-8b80-569c-9c95-98f9405e5eca" :question/math-academy-id "q-159311" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)({y}^{2}-1)}{x{y}^{2}}, & x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 2$ and $y\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{(x-2)({y}^{2}-1)}{x{y}^{2}}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-2}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-2}{x}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}, & x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527177 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1544ee01-f534-5f91-a989-82f6c4f8f30f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526544} {:answer/id #uuid "3ade37f7-6413-5d0c-aff6-f6ec6bccd435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526614} {:answer/id #uuid "3c40ec30-1fb8-58f2-8c9a-e6bd665ac483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526615} {:answer/id #uuid "a12ca628-2589-5dfb-9c58-44d876d2d743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526816} {:answer/id #uuid "a54b7301-a104-5d35-8c4d-e29e0811b8dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526818} :answer-field/id #uuid "661c9719-3dbb-5353-ab1d-1d0798143c7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "86ba6c5d-d640-5e57-88b7-9b3f13232b7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159337" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y}{2\\pi }, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>2,\\,y\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{X}(x),$ the marginal CDF of $X,$ for $0\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=F(x,\\infty )=\\lim_{y\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le x\\le 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & =\\lim_{y\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y}{2\\pi }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\pi }\\cdot {lim}_{y\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527178 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18626b8c-2493-5d55-9463-bd9f28fb30c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526554} {:answer/id #uuid "1adf42e3-1c70-5503-860d-6962e69cc281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526560} {:answer/id #uuid "22d83ace-ef87-55ef-a2c4-9b139c05acd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526577} {:answer/id #uuid "d5612858-da5d-5749-b4be-3afeccbaa11a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526913} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f6b48f-b583-53c7-8275-d83bc842c8f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526919}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526560} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4504051-feaa-512b-9211-403c240f5138" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "86df2b6f-b298-58e2-a359-d3e3b918ef94" :question/math-academy-id "q-111250" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>Y).$" :question/worked-solution "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,2]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A=\\{X>Y\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/2d/2db85950b4d45901d79c173f264173d0c38046b2f087d0a28c8d5c69f024e31f.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>Y) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{A}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48289cb0-2db9-541c-ba9b-d4327439a579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526645} {:answer/id #uuid "626f8019-c00b-5e2b-9eae-917eb16fcd3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526700} {:answer/id #uuid "77cd1431-7dea-5816-88cc-589a8ce57085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526737} {:answer/id #uuid "ce683808-6c11-54ae-bb49-f99568096871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526898} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5a9688-f2d5-553e-872b-13b2824acaba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526973}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526645} :answer-field/id #uuid "92fcb5de-d0cd-5bb2-911a-0615ed463dd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9a19ca23-6c60-5d6e-8799-38d55529cc16" :question/math-academy-id "q-143871" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[{(X+Y)}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & y\\le x\\le 4-y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\le x\\le 4-y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/8d/8d1935944ffa0017da736fc540ff823df8a052c2c32c985553cde08ea5fce8da.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{(X+Y)}^{2}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}(x+y)^{2}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)^{2}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}(x+y)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}(x+y)^{3}]}_{x=y}^{x=4-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\frac{1}{3}(4-y+y)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}(y+y)^{3}]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({4}^{3}-(2y)^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(64-8{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{[64y-2{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(128-32) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "419508b7-b598-5bac-aefc-71912a4034c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526631} {:answer/id #uuid "52e29e0e-3acf-5e78-b135-92a31a0d1a0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526669} {:answer/id #uuid "a5bb0ace-70a3-52eb-95ec-8d06773149b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526820} {:answer/id #uuid "b2538d50-74dd-5675-80ce-ae08eaba9106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e-1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526844} {:answer/id #uuid "fda9eb6e-8342-5d4d-a8b8-51e569d33bde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(e-1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526820} :answer-field/id #uuid "b806adf7-55c6-5e96-8591-27f8f3de0558" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ba35bb1-196e-51b8-b71e-102fe895702c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143884" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[{e}^{X-Y}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)={e}^{x-y}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{e}^{X-Y}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}{e}^{x-y}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x-y}\\cdot {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[{e}^{x}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(e-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[(e-1)y]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =(e-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15aa0740-bb6f-50d4-a937-2834a1c7e53c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{x{e}^{-2y}}{2+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526547} {:answer/id #uuid "9664a9c8-6635-5edb-95e2-cc6d28ff1c9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2+x}(1-{e}^{-2y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526797} {:answer/id #uuid "b69b91e9-4e20-5e6f-b2c3-445a82748c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{1+x}(1-{e}^{-2y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526854} {:answer/id #uuid "e34a28f2-4fe3-5a59-b680-84b2b53ea3c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{x}{2+x}-{e}^{-2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526948} {:answer/id #uuid "f54531dc-c152-57ba-b9ee-ccc647ffc034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2y}}{2+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526989}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526797} :answer-field/id #uuid "55ed3e7c-fab6-5103-98c8-80e32b0d59cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a27194f1-04ae-54c8-8fbb-2f24bc3ed222" :question/math-academy-id "q-159322" :question/problem "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2+x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-2y}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{x}{2+x}\\right)(1-{e}^{-2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2+x}(1-{e}^{-2y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2+x}(1-{e}^{-2y}), & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a3f1677-b272-57cb-bfa3-3f5d3ab81a1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526525} {:answer/id #uuid "0f8acf17-693a-53fb-9034-d0608ef3c3b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526534} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc14b6f-032f-51fe-a8fa-be5878a1d00c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526662} {:answer/id #uuid "68a4acc5-d960-59ae-98ec-17c1c59437b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}(2{x}^{2}+{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526712} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb7fa80-1aa3-58ec-a7a7-78c251d5440b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526865}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526662} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6d562ef-bf77-5294-b08b-2567310b5dfd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b3af2400-05b8-5370-91fa-551e055c25be" :question/math-academy-id "q-159318" :question/problem "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>2\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & y>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\text{ or }y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y>2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3}, & x>2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>2,\\,y>2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a8298a5-fac1-5365-b909-1315a53989cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526687} {:answer/id #uuid "b3de5092-4946-5d3e-b8a7-ad0de03a3d23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526847} {:answer/id #uuid "b4aaf98a-33ad-537d-a3f0-b7504ddda61e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526850} {:answer/id #uuid "ec09683f-97e8-5921-86c5-63360bce02d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526971} {:answer/id #uuid "fc18d19d-57df-5431-9db5-d3ea0a3393eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527003} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e342586-6b50-54ec-9bac-5940a5f9cadc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b5d80cd0-76ca-5260-b15c-cf7c6e9faf1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-146309" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}xy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the covariance between $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle \n\n$$\nS=[0,2]\\times [0,4].\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=g(x)\\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $h(y)=\\frac{y}{8}.$ \n\nTherefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0094f649-619d-53d4-bd4a-599974a79f65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526499} {:answer/id #uuid "0b87866d-7ca3-5ffe-9bf1-7ca2dbed6087" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526527} {:answer/id #uuid "617c7900-1787-5067-bf53-b914ea33d964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526697} {:answer/id #uuid "88871071-0592-5a72-9ee4-9896fb71c1b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526774} {:answer/id #uuid "d153fc31-a51b-5ff0-a56d-5400a28d1dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526906}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526906} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a370ffb-9f03-59b2-ac60-62d599bab917" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "da99206a-ee96-54b9-a8bb-1c95438c5559" :question/math-academy-id "q-143850" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[4(X-1)],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=4(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/bd/bd8d2d36a0fe570e6ccdb096c6c39bc9c9edeed9b008dada504801025055438f.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[4(X-1)] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}4(x-1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4(x-1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{{x}^{2}}4(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-1){\\int }_{1}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-1)[y]_{y=1}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-1)({x}^{2}-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-x+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =3[(4-\\frac{8}{3}-2+2)-(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+1)] \\\\  & =3(\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{5}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ac938f-f798-5038-bab5-fa297deba0a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526502} {:answer/id #uuid "03d36e20-40e6-5b80-9c0d-67f894b58f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{24}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526509} {:answer/id #uuid "254d8e8c-0380-5000-b8d0-a054397414b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{24}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526585} {:answer/id #uuid "b225b894-490f-581e-bce4-4a2a39058d58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{128}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526843} {:answer/id #uuid "e29f4e96-d27a-5c57-bc4c-2abd5d1dceda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{128}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526946}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526843} :answer-field/id #uuid "35729131-6ecd-51d1-af5b-b92e2a8eb812" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e1859419-9341-5ec9-877c-8ef2835f890f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159323" :question/problem "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}, & x\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 2$ and $y\\ge 2.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 2$ and $y\\ge 2,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}})(1-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{128}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{128}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}, & x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58ac6aa4-3187-51f4-8986-37a77eb9556e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526684} {:answer/id #uuid "67d3f414-e5a3-5a85-86ff-7be8a7d0d430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526708} {:answer/id #uuid "6ddc4b8b-fe6b-5cab-a0b5-82d769645de3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526718} {:answer/id #uuid "b6768082-39bc-5d53-bad6-15fd36ca45b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526853} {:answer/id #uuid "cfcead82-4acf-5200-8fd5-75bb552b7196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526901}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526901} :answer-field/id #uuid "719de611-fe3d-510b-8ec3-a7dea6d0ecc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f125a564-9e7e-50c1-b77c-f6cbd0ae6169" :question/math-academy-id "q-143879" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}y+x{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{y{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{3}{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{y}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{8}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+2-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "575f89b2-b860-5bf6-94c2-b2386d5174b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{3{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526682} {:answer/id #uuid "ac47ec72-2d41-5b5a-b068-bbdef9147797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{3{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526834} {:answer/id #uuid "dd56e164-faac-55a7-ae60-d5adf3110db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16{x}^{3}}{9{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526935} {:answer/id #uuid "fa973044-c950-5daa-a2a0-d7518f5a10c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16x}{3{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527000} {:answer/id #uuid "fe148dd0-c4b8-52c9-9a1c-2693969bc24e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{9{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527008}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527000} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bb56fd2-9bf7-57d4-98c0-5fd980b76e4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f97149b0-2afa-55ad-bcc3-a7b6ba9e10a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-159331" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}({y}^{2}-1)}{3{y}^{2}}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y>2 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>1,\\,y>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{4{x}^{2}({y}^{2}-1)}{3{y}^{2}}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3{y}^{3}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{16x}{3{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16x}{3{y}^{3}}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236a2d72-bb0b-50be-bba3-e4d52248a15f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526581} {:answer/id #uuid "4b66b758-0826-5108-9ac0-ade8eefbc63c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526657} {:answer/id #uuid "71dd7185-fad4-53e7-b17c-bea3ed88b7f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526723} {:answer/id #uuid "b08f83bf-773c-562d-b48f-e6657ce17368" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526838} {:answer/id #uuid "d77ff831-4178-5cb0-8b9c-9a40136d4cb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526918}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526838} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0c66bcf-f118-5a15-8c75-354e4b9c987f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fcac8514-54e9-5652-a542-ae9e241a741a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159315" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)(y-1)}{xy}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{(x-1)(y-1)}{xy}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "483c1d43-041f-5678-ab9f-50bec83d7f8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,2,-4)$ and   $(1,1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527449} {:answer/id #uuid "69a4522f-18fd-5114-a1be-146db5b750da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,3,-3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527508} {:answer/id #uuid "983f6f82-3266-53f7-b95d-ce0f1f0ffea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-3,-9)$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527616} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f5a05c-1273-59a1-bc11-b4fb2a55699d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1,-7)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527735} {:answer/id #uuid "f7bd770f-a8c7-568c-bde9-a4473018a372" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4,4-2)$  and   $(0,2,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527788}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527508} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a8d8bc9-45fa-5d95-aee6-2a55394b5faf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0c6c3541-8949-51d1-86fa-598cbd047050" :question/math-academy-id "q-94780" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2{z}^{2}$ and the line $x=y=z+6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\nx=y=z+6=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=t \\\\ z=t-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2{z}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+{t}^{2} & =2(t-6)^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+{t}^{2} & =2({t}^{2}-12t+36) \\\\ 2{t}^{2} & =2{t}^{2}-24t+72 \\\\ 24t & =72 \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solution back into the equations of the line. For $t=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3 \\\\ y=3 \\\\ z=3-6=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(3,3-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19a8bec2-48ba-5a11-a243-ad1cfdb0c57b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527363} {:answer/id #uuid "7fc5004c-bab4-52e2-ace0-ae6162e110d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527554} {:answer/id #uuid "b8227aeb-2f23-5441-8fc0-8c3dbb03d4b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527687} {:answer/id #uuid "de737258-a686-5bef-bdde-8f04025a1062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527749} {:answer/id #uuid "fcad7b2f-d2c4-56a4-a9ff-d30db2074f4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527796}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527796} :answer-field/id #uuid "c53ed922-aca5-5bca-99bf-697d183ea725" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "315997a1-7d2c-5dee-aeca-23b06ca1daaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-146201" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}, & x=5 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}, & x=10 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=2]$ given that the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)$ is shown above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|2}=8.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)]-{\\mu }_{X|2}^{2} \\\\  & =[(5)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}+(10)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}]-(8)^{2} \\\\  & =10+60-64 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527945 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a403057-49da-5281-95e9-2b61e1f6c156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4,2,2)$ and $(8,4,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527579} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc1d652-8f64-55d9-a0a5-6794fcdf58b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4,2,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527586} {:answer/id #uuid "a19965fa-cbd9-5c65-bf6b-ca13371cc307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(4,2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527640} {:answer/id #uuid "ce32204a-bd90-5a02-95bb-9e251b7948af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(8,4,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527726} {:answer/id #uuid "d755c9c6-8859-50b7-b00f-25201a9e2e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(8,4,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527741}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527640} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b76cfe8-40cb-5f07-be72-0076c0000916" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5a9b6c0a-2d84-570a-8860-6a6e61ceeed3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94084" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=8{z}^{2}$ and the line $\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{2}=z-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{2}=z-1=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4t \\\\ y=2t \\\\ z=t+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2} & =8{z}^{2} \\\\ (4t)^{2}+4(2t)^{2} & =8(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 16{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2} & =8(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 32{t}^{2} & =8(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 4{t}^{2} & =(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 4{t}^{2} & ={t}^{2}+2t+1 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-2t-1 & =0 \\\\ (3t+1)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{3},1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y=2\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ z=-\\frac{1}{3}+1=\\frac{2}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).\n$$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4(1)=4 \\\\ y=2(1)=2 \\\\ z=1+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(4,2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(4,2,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527950 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dd6de30-6014-5a8c-958c-74bee530a51f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527336} {:answer/id #uuid "10a9c696-6e92-5cef-a6d2-a7fbbfa35df6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527343} {:answer/id #uuid "67f5954e-0888-5e41-aa25-993cc635c802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527504} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb54557-388a-55ab-b4f8-52bc95263e1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527721} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb2a9d4-480e-5425-b3fa-01ec4e2f3b34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527725}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527721} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eca249b-fcf1-5eef-8bb3-bbd4ab981b54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "611fada0-4ebb-55c6-bcaf-8dccb0c75bca" :question/math-academy-id "q-146208" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,8)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.45, & y=4 \\\\ 0.55, & y=6 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=8]$ given that the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,8)$ is shown above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|8}=5.1.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=8] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,8)]-{\\mu }_{Y|8}^{2} \\\\  & =[(4)^{2}\\cdot 0.45+(6)^{2}\\cdot 0.55]-(5.1)^{2} \\\\  & =7.2+19.8-26.01 \\\\  & =0.99.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "310fc6a4-db62-5f96-8df4-bbd6b181d385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527410} {:answer/id #uuid "87176957-585a-58b1-ba8a-7520d939ae2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527571} {:answer/id #uuid "b8cfef22-7523-58f7-9f2e-991730673fa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527688} {:answer/id #uuid "cceac401-e703-5120-be30-1c346fcae5ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527722} {:answer/id #uuid "f53eb1ee-a782-5e0d-ad7e-d9c94d71e493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527785}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527785} :answer-field/id #uuid "079de692-aa36-527f-b17f-d2c9a0e53c01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9f6a4dc1-cc0e-50e8-a876-123c929a6728" :question/math-academy-id "q-146202" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.4, & x=1 \\\\ 0.6, & x=2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=1]$ given that the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)$ is shown above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|1}=1.6.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)]-{\\mu }_{X|1}^{2} \\\\  & =[(1)^{2}\\cdot 0.4+(2)^{2}\\cdot 0.6]-(1.6)^{2} \\\\  & =0.4+2.4-2.56 \\\\  & =0.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530417 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa63b29-0409-5b18-83ab-8b4023056556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529596} {:answer/id #uuid "7a9c8d26-f55d-57d5-82b3-aed3370cefe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529891} {:answer/id #uuid "c72df470-55dc-5ffb-9294-61de19e9549f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{54}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530081} {:answer/id #uuid "cd39717a-740f-51a4-af35-8d0e263f4e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530095} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7eacc8-0ca8-5f80-8673-05ce828c5aa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{144}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530128}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530128} :answer-field/id #uuid "72b99b6c-e8bf-5475-94ee-9d603ea83a76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "04b02bf9-cae6-594f-ac96-340afba92b08" :question/math-academy-id "q-149411" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,-1)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4{y}^{13/4}}, & \\,1\\le y \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=-1]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,-1)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|-1}=\\frac{9}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=-1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{4{y}^{13/4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{4{y}^{5/4}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{4}{y}^{-5/4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot (-4)\\cdot {y}^{-1/4}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{9}{\\sqrt[4]{y}}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =0-(-9) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=-2] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=-2]-{\\mu }_{Y|-2}^{2} \\\\  & =9-{\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =9-\\frac{81}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09127fa6-751e-5de5-9626-f7c3ade21701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529594} {:answer/id #uuid "6fbd7798-2750-5c2f-b9ba-11c058833061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529867}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529594} :answer-field/id #uuid "52e5d219-347a-59f7-9240-fa2c72d0c7a8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530279} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0adbae6f-09ad-5e85-adc2-3211170c6c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529597} {:answer/id #uuid "15eece98-8f78-5bc6-9ed2-aea0eb111e1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529623} {:answer/id #uuid "61ca4b5b-b2d8-5291-94f1-c7eb2d49d57c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529820}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529820} :answer-field/id #uuid "6382c6a6-f666-5da1-8304-c0b7d94b708d" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530294} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42d82ed7-2abe-5218-805f-fcc5eb486615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529735} {:answer/id #uuid "5cdb7e83-38c8-50b7-a49b-da58cbf269d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529805} {:answer/id #uuid "80971667-9e1b-5176-97ce-f66f67a28c00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529900} {:answer/id #uuid "c762e497-f089-56cb-9742-afadfb762306" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530082}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530082} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a10a52-6833-53cc-ba9f-c7f4b8bbeab2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530336} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e536d67-208f-5109-860d-8ce8e8456549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529607} {:answer/id #uuid "125c2c98-2434-5932-a480-2510a1b6f6d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529607} :answer-field/id #uuid "d09acdef-394b-56b7-aede-21eb94c695dd" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "21116522-1e68-5aeb-8229-3d0039b396bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-333649" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-8x(t)+3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$\n$y=x$ is {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-2}} is {{field-3}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=2x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=2x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/33/330e2a1dd9f7d2cbcadb61fe49e767d95ee76a2cb669a9917ef3f6333719b98f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5db52af3-2d00-57fe-83ac-e1e4cabb425b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529806} {:answer/id #uuid "6716a6d3-11e8-5185-8a14-c81cf63f513b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529837} {:answer/id #uuid "a5022d84-3876-5770-bcec-f22b051278c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529988} {:answer/id #uuid "cde47949-e5a4-500e-82a8-40c11888b0e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530097} {:answer/id #uuid "d6596022-5d1b-577f-a508-e5cb0a327c6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529837} :answer-field/id #uuid "7186124f-248a-5608-b06b-90a979a094ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2866f3dd-6eaf-53a1-aed5-f4ad68a3ab1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-146395" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=1]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|1}=\\frac{2}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot 2y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=1]-{\\mu }_{Y|1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{4}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7382e9bb-66e3-536f-bf47-96a851f09b48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{384}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529874} {:answer/id #uuid "88e834ca-6b87-553a-a237-a4681d65ed06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{381}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529912} {:answer/id #uuid "a56625c4-5e98-548c-bea6-5d94c2fb908e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{54}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529992} {:answer/id #uuid "cb89ee2c-2a49-5be9-9e3b-ad09f4a34f3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{51}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530091} {:answer/id #uuid "e6cbeb1a-1375-5455-b8f6-f5ce904eaab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{360}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530157}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529992} :answer-field/id #uuid "de48ce71-ed4e-5a50-80fd-461f4a5c82ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3b4c1a29-e4bc-58eb-ae8a-170f49cecb2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-146761" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,0)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}\\sqrt{x}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=0]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,0)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|0}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=0] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{14}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{3}{14}{x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{49}{x}^{7/2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{49}(128-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{381}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=0] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=0]-{\\mu }_{X|0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{381}{49}-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{7}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{381}{49}-\\frac{3}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{378}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{54}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2466ac54-a28c-5e81-8865-e657fdfdddf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529657} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8a7671-a4f9-5cb6-aad5-295efc3dd7c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1+2t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2t-1){e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529768} {:answer/id #uuid "6015db17-0453-5d51-9f6c-9b63c750a7e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529813} {:answer/id #uuid "7d81a220-377a-5acf-8093-4fcf21b38c96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ -(1+2t){e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529896} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ea50a5-4a7b-5fa7-80c3-71388673c51f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1+\\frac{t}{2}){e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2t-1){e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529813} :answer-field/id #uuid "15c8b6c9-4d90-5de4-bd62-0285c561ccb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4a2a9a41-3861-52b6-aa0d-2940105366a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-80616" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2+2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}-1{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $$\n\\displaystyle x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ -{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ -{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530459 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23beb7dd-cb27-516a-87c2-22187aecddb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529656} {:answer/id #uuid "694824b2-68f4-5131-a788-8727d4006841" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529847} {:answer/id #uuid "98abe252-a017-5e77-b742-868d2adf14cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529954} {:answer/id #uuid "a16fd62d-5444-5e85-96f9-c5d6c7412063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529980} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d991b4-30f6-52a1-afce-0bb0222c6615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{48}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529847} :answer-field/id #uuid "dccf4473-8ce6-5a7b-9170-9d177e05cbfd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "59aca6d9-1752-57b6-a727-2d8a95f6602b" :question/math-academy-id "q-149357" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,0)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{7{x}^{5/2}}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=0]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,0)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|0}=\\frac{12}{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=0] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{12}{7{x}^{5/2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{12}{7\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{24\\sqrt{x}}{7}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{7}-\\frac{24}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=0] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=0]-{\\mu }_{X|0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{7}-{\\left(\\frac{12}{7}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{7}-\\frac{144}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34cc357a-5d15-5858-a9da-1fbed5a44025" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529702} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7acdc3-b79c-540c-8876-d8d4ab0dc5e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529807} {:answer/id #uuid "6726955f-fcbe-51ef-81c8-c5b97a0a797b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529838} {:answer/id #uuid "78434c08-58fd-5864-98a8-e08d7e0b4b36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529886} {:answer/id #uuid "dda5e757-29ae-51d9-ac4e-1c47bef47985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530129}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530129} :answer-field/id #uuid "06e3e1ec-1b29-5e88-912e-1d47aa3d4d7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6104314b-b213-5403-b96a-2e0ddf20d4ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-149399" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,5)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}, & \\,x\\le -1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=5]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,5)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|5}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=5] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}3{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-3\\cdot {x}^{-1}]}_{-\\infty }^{-1} \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{3}{x}]}_{-\\infty }^{-1} \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=5] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=5]-{\\mu }_{X|5}^{2} \\\\  & =3-{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1489093e-e3a3-55f2-b8ea-f74432755e95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529620} {:answer/id #uuid "15dde4cf-3e00-5078-bcf3-4598e3e2c37b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529622} {:answer/id #uuid "a86c312b-7c26-5fa7-90ee-febf5e5c1ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529997} {:answer/id #uuid "b64ece9b-7dd8-5d15-a949-4a704a08d316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530036} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a364ff-fd0c-5130-ba2f-e616cd755bc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530192}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530192} :answer-field/id #uuid "78d192f2-fd61-50da-beb1-1728d446e7ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8cb1a10c-eb8d-5503-81eb-a44381451337" :question/math-academy-id "q-146807" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{{x}^{3/2}}, & \\,4\\le x\\le 9 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=4]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|4}=6.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=4] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{9}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{x}^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{9}3\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3/2}]}_{4}^{9} \\\\  & =2(27-8) \\\\  & =38.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=4] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=4]-{\\mu }_{X|4}^{2} \\\\  & =38-{6}^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37ac5a0b-682d-58ce-8d19-548dbcc7f74d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529706} {:answer/id #uuid "4afaae21-7107-5bfd-ad67-092f29f09b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529758} {:answer/id #uuid "89f5dd1b-92ab-5512-a96e-3f46933c72fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529916} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea6f873-9f56-50db-b46b-34cc9aa92655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529972} {:answer/id #uuid "d83f26db-9776-52f9-ae64-5454e381d5e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530117} :answer-field/id #uuid "c24a3477-bac3-594c-9727-d90bcb135154" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "988b2bee-8b04-51ca-a9c5-c53fb0470471" :question/math-academy-id "q-149406" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,-4)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2{x}^{9/2}}, & \\,x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=-4]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,-4)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|-4}=\\frac{7}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=-4] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2{x}^{9/2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{5/2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\cdot {x}^{-3/2}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{7}{3{x}^{3/2}}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =0-\\left(-\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=-4] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=-4]-{\\mu }_{X|-4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}-\\frac{49}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530500 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31af8209-d6f7-5861-bb8f-000ec6cd874e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}(t-1){e}^{5t} \\\\ 2{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529690} {:answer/id #uuid "6bed8f55-cb39-5a3b-86c2-5cd8c26c256c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2+t){e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529854} {:answer/id #uuid "a90db176-5114-5ca8-a22e-ebcadf82891a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}(t-1) \\\\ (2-t){e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529998} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ce04dc-14c5-5800-b05b-94c7bf45b6c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}(2t-1){e}^{5t} \\\\ (t+2){e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530038} {:answer/id #uuid "d8570b37-63eb-524c-ba0e-1892354b0a05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530118}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529854} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d46ab32-dd48-56fe-af69-855cd332f49c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a4894a4b-386a-5db4-8e7b-0e56f6d08b5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80617" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5-5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5-5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $$\n\\displaystyle x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}. \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2+t){e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd913af-0d4d-584f-bb88-dda0c4ba49dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{63}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529640} {:answer/id #uuid "491be78e-8f5c-5fda-ad13-feacd90c83d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529752} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1fdd49-b96a-5691-8015-a399cf64cdd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529760} {:answer/id #uuid "bc42e7b8-acd5-5885-b965-56dae0313424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530051} {:answer/id #uuid "e09649d9-9ba1-578c-809b-de4e41e98be6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530139}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530051} :answer-field/id #uuid "c39ce385-0fcd-519a-a701-e719a952cd46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a618ef53-1eb4-5d7e-994a-cd43bde62157" :question/math-academy-id "q-146771" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,-1)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{24}{7{x}^{4}}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=-1]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,-1)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|-1}=\\frac{9}{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=-1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{24}{7{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{24}{7{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{7}{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{7}[\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-(-1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=-1]-{\\mu }_{X|-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{7}-{\\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{7}-\\frac{81}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fb0d6fb-00d5-5de1-a313-8844185c3c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529684} {:answer/id #uuid "33418197-c818-5634-8bea-8cd2f392480a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529693} {:answer/id #uuid "3451d803-efd4-5f81-986a-440c80249e19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529699} {:answer/id #uuid "77877244-7515-5435-983f-d36e7965ce08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529883} {:answer/id #uuid "eafb00d8-0e70-5b61-9556-8adc6cb3395b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530168}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529883} :answer-field/id #uuid "649ea598-2503-50e9-9e7d-932e638f034c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aa39286d-2046-5d16-b60c-2e22bf1173b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-149407" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,6)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{7}}, & \\,x\\le -1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=6]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,6)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|6}=-\\frac{6}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=6] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{7}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}-6{x}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{(-6)}{(-4)}\\cdot {x}^{-4}]}_{-\\infty }^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2{x}^{4}}]}_{-\\infty }^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=6] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=6]-{\\mu }_{X|6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-{\\left(-\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{36}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6123a8e7-8566-539c-a378-251b6266a004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529818} {:answer/id #uuid "afc1305e-c6de-5db9-8835-27482372478c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530019} {:answer/id #uuid "dd60fa24-2113-573f-927a-cc5cc09eff1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530126} {:answer/id #uuid "f239d215-8bad-5494-ad9f-a7e4dc4827bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530182} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb02d5f-3761-5753-87fa-efd0f4de2d82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530214}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530214} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ffbd89f-d981-524e-9095-fdb0dc1768f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c42bb2d4-33f1-5601-a069-ea5fef63539f" :question/math-academy-id "q-326237" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{4t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4-4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4-4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{4t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{5},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9108600b-92c8-5cac-8349-d5b082ccbc72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529932} {:answer/id #uuid "ea06fb62-1823-5c87-b12d-1aaa69446262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530165}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529932} :answer-field/id #uuid "067d8b08-6895-5be5-a9f6-a2b1e3667c90" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530220} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cac2988-a080-595f-843f-97eb5958cbbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529602} {:answer/id #uuid "1c231f71-e7d0-538f-b96d-5930e103e800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529636} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b1ce46-65e7-5567-a0a6-4ebda43fe0c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530047}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530047} :answer-field/id #uuid "5eab285f-c597-546c-aa55-410e9e2e5d6f" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530289} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ee89a1e-68f4-5ee3-bf52-c052f8a4875b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529645} {:answer/id #uuid "b840c1f0-2b0c-58d2-be26-ac4a951a3ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530040}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530040} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d2e6217-2c75-5d5b-a56a-86f1ab8b7813" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530301} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e311032-7034-5ca1-84d6-f1d9adb98503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529679} {:answer/id #uuid "8a40f91d-daa6-5f8c-9c8d-0263fa5d7838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529918} {:answer/id #uuid "a393adc3-4827-5198-a81a-abd84742a4b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529985} {:answer/id #uuid "c629958a-0bea-5ec4-9abc-ab8b41352160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530075}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529918} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc482d57-60ea-5929-a93c-02bf101b157d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d325e43f-35f1-5542-8db8-397925f79316" :question/math-academy-id "q-333656" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$\n$y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-2}} is {{field-3}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/e5/e5c236defa66a00f12cd338ed0012cf91e4d7c15aa4865fc2b1edf5b0e2ea1da.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c30eff0d-ffd1-5d17-bc6f-a345783aae21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1+t){e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530067} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4d4ff6-2575-5aa5-adaf-1dfeb10d5b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-2t){e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530102} {:answer/id #uuid "db46ec5a-9287-56d8-98f8-2de1ee93eb52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530121} {:answer/id #uuid "e582a2bf-4e2c-5f80-aa69-4386efa886fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-2t){e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530155} {:answer/id #uuid "e930397d-0699-53ec-a3a5-3a49c82b1c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530161}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530161} :answer-field/id #uuid "80a2d0fe-c382-5881-8e02-d1c86d71cb09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d67f8554-75f7-5018-9643-79d6cf0e83b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-80929" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =-3.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2+3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4+3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}+{w}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -{w}_{1}-{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $$\n\\displaystyle x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={e}^{-3t}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "731aa6f4-9b42-511b-99eb-f375784ef6ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531119}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531119} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c17466c-b8ca-50c3-9f75-a6a53610a6c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531464} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26d00d5e-9f6b-5691-9183-ca640417b15f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530951}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530951} :answer-field/id #uuid "79975f54-a6ab-5d67-8f15-fd4af267d146" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2e73d348-8d9b-5a9f-8dbf-5305290f7f53" :question/math-academy-id "q-332913" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+$ {{field-1}} $\\cdot \\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=-3{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ -3\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3a \\\\ -3b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2b \\\\ 2a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3a \\\\ -3b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2b+1 \\\\ 2a+5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2b+1=-3a \\\\ 2a+5=-3b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n-2b+1=-3a\\,⇒\\,a=\\frac{2b-1}{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n2\\left(\\frac{2b-1}{3}\\right)+5=-3b\\,⇒\\,4b-2+15=-9b\\,⇒\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\na=\\frac{2(-1)-1}{3}=\\frac{-2-1}{3}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76419d8d-c305-5619-a8a2-c0287e5dda0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531126}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531126} :answer-field/id #uuid "2933141c-2169-5b87-8fb5-b8ed2e025c9c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531415} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cafae102-8ddc-57ce-b89b-eb060527e40e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531288} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f12c9a5-b2a0-5f55-8200-bc2b6ab50775" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531496} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d157cb9-9968-5484-a3c7-ab43310ab864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531202}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531202} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3dc74ec-4f14-5ce0-aef2-9920a7e3da3c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3234de52-8801-51a2-806f-23f965c5de2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333012" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{2}+t+3 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+2t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$\\,\\,{t}^{2}+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$2$\n  </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n    .\n  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$1$\n  </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{2}+t+3 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+2t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{2}+t+3 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-({a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}) \\\\ {a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{2}+t+3 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-{a}_{2}+2){t}^{2}+(-{b}_{2}+1)t+(-{c}_{2}+3) \\\\ ({a}_{1}-1){t}^{2}+({b}_{1}+2)t+{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coefficients of like powers of $t,$ we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{2}+2=0 \\\\ -\\,{b}_{2}+1=2{a}_{1} \\\\ -\\,{c}_{2}+3={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1}-1=0 \\\\ {b}_{1}+2=2{a}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}={b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first and fourth equations, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=2,\\,{a}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the second and fifth equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=-1,\\,{b}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these last values into the third and sixth equations, and solving for ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=1,\\,{c}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+2t-1 \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-t+1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "231f5d7d-ba14-58c8-9817-e96238be05d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530940}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530940} :answer-field/id #uuid "0975e181-89ef-5188-9ed5-faa802b35aa0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531390} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e07e33b3-ea76-5f2d-8458-b5bf6baa03bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531322}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531322} :answer-field/id #uuid "304765d0-077d-5553-8e0b-02a9e5acf013" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531424} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cf26826-fc27-58f2-b062-1e875c147069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530964}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530964} :answer-field/id #uuid "38b786bc-2f8e-50dc-9e4a-f05c7bbe489c" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531433} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2b5958f-36f6-5197-b67d-199edfefbc79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531359} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ad21e9c-b394-53e0-abac-39126c2eaed6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531462} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "858cb189-c87b-5c12-8cae-08db96e24407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531155}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531155} :answer-field/id #uuid "be99c1cf-9606-5cac-85ca-8bcf7bfdd5d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531512} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ae49c4e-218f-5613-b105-b87baac9b7d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531133}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531133} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf969b04-1973-578a-b415-7226b4cb20bd" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531514} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef96fb33-f115-54df-9b44-caf8bfa136a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531349}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531349} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed1cb951-e683-519c-9741-c07df8a9e235" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531542} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e2f551a-2dab-5878-8d7b-9b4786b3123a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530995}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530995} :answer-field/id #uuid "eedf4d51-ddf3-5e05-8956-364b54b00bc4" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "48e63c6e-e110-5f2f-9c73-cb4374b7c6c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-332587" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-4{x}_{2}(t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe systems of linear ODEs above can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}^{′}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,x(t)+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-5}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-6}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-7}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-8}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=1\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-4)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531594 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae3f5928-02ba-596c-85cb-7d15890d6276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531242}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531242} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cbb24e4-e2ab-54ec-bf66-effde280b307" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531394} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92982e2f-61eb-50b9-809b-23ee5a048d58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531180}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531180} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cadcc96-5961-586a-8a05-aa4c6a2de9f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531409} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a525e594-c221-5dcc-979b-ca3551e65f27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531220}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531220} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f7c1dd5-f9b0-5de5-b21c-aa047db00ae5" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531411} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bae07a31-0fc0-55e4-ad14-7e86f2d59d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531260}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531260} :answer-field/id #uuid "859292aa-c590-5355-97a7-05b34bf3e722" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531477} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd463e34-178a-54a6-8919-1d5e22381036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531379}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531379} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc291a7a-ba58-599b-9c16-d930c364406a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531521} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c0fe51c-df57-5055-a519-b8b8b55b9733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531026}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531026} :answer-field/id #uuid "d86cd08e-2e74-5dfa-8aed-573414a8abcf" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531530} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f06ff225-35f1-5cdc-9f88-0f66df868c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531353}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531353} :answer-field/id #uuid "dae87d1f-b8bd-55e1-96e2-67ce502bd32d" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531533} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3086aa8-bdb0-5979-96ad-3d44946c7eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531301}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531301} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff58c76f-d503-592c-8830-047604f3389f" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "56f780de-76f1-5a1a-81ae-abc4bc467c3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332576" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)+5{e}^{4t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=7{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+6{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system of linear ODEs above can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}^{′}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,x(t)+{e}^{4t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+\\,{e}^{5t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-5}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-6}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-7}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-8}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=(-2)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 5+{e}^{5t}\\cdot 0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+1\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 0+{e}^{5t}\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531652 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5af1a84-1b56-54b2-b7ce-8c0d34ffda38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531365} :answer-field/id #uuid "1524671b-66ed-568b-a1ea-32c1c8729504" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531401} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95276f1c-36f0-5f08-904e-b3c36535d237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531187}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531187} :answer-field/id #uuid "1be54abc-d2f8-5e71-86b4-95e3ede8e8b9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531408} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "776ac9c0-5765-58c1-ae99-c312dfc60e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531129}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531129} :answer-field/id #uuid "39227e03-733f-5344-954d-3c31f3502f19" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531434} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2f832c5-576b-5b01-8a76-3c3ab8a327a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531218} :answer-field/id #uuid "393e1770-3ee3-5e3e-950e-08a227d928cc" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531435} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb1585ad-6c25-559d-a87d-5cb17b1bf0fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531375}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531375} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f6f012a-2c3a-5777-891a-7086389514fb" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531441} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f2a7653-2cc3-5d49-8385-0aabe06fc0eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531209}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531209} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aed8437-ee45-5890-83bb-09c9f9e1bcc8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531454} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "376594fc-6236-56e8-b6ba-46f5e5522edc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530984}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530984} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e3bde12-1083-598d-9f01-6622ee8cbb56" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531480} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e52aaec-6699-5c73-9493-b586f9420d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531111}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531111} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5d80446-5433-569c-ad43-d2633b905967" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eadcb6eb-28e0-561c-baf8-c3576858d2b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-332585" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe systems of linear ODEs above can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}^{′}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,x(t)+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-5}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-6}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-7}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-8}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=(-3)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+7\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-1)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(-2)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4899d2f0-4a9a-54c0-b255-243f00296fb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x-z,\\,$ $1,\\,$  and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532082} {:answer/id #uuid "7307529a-c5fa-50c3-85e4-0c320e676b56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$yz,\\,$ $1,\\,$  and $z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532192} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3d9025-0e2b-5e52-b2d1-c27a27ac63c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2z,\\,$ $0,\\,$  and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532212} {:answer/id #uuid "900204f4-4ee6-51fa-bded-94c975db4cc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2z,\\,$ $1,\\,$  and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532260} {:answer/id #uuid "d5255563-50f7-52ae-bccd-46caade6840e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$xz,\\,$ $0,\\,$  and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532390}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532082} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2718901-61ad-591c-ba8b-c2899ee3b35f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "01a5e642-9803-5a1b-8846-85c812cf1513" :question/math-academy-id "q-162815" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)=xy-zy$ on the intersection of the surfaces $y+z=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,-y⟩=\\lambda ⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ y+z=1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy-zy\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=y+z=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨y,\\,x-z,\\,-y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80c94ff5-e094-5fec-a663-783e3db82388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9493" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532225}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532225} :answer-field/id #uuid "efc50829-ddbd-58f0-8a60-4f7a72a20ec5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a330685-db48-5728-bb5b-a1b5a0526e38" :question/math-academy-id "q-332929" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.63),$ compute $P(X<4).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places.\n\n$P(X<4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.63),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.37)}^{x-1}(0.63), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(0.37)}^{0}(0.63)+{(0.37)}^{1}(0.63)+{(0.37)}^{2}(0.63) \\\\  & ≈0.9493.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1426c5a-cb34-58ac-9b8e-e7c4a43242b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532324}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532324} :answer-field/id #uuid "065b0803-ec37-52f0-b035-55be85dd7364" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532493} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f7c4fa-35f3-5ea1-bf8d-567b0e9bd672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531987}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531987} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a8aefaa-4fc2-5f99-957b-9d1dc03b153e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3860fd8d-fa51-5495-8869-9f057f53d9a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-250073" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}$ and the line $y=9x-14$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ Find the values of ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}.$\n\n${y}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{y}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} \\\\ y=9x-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =9x-14 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9x+14 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-7) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=7.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the parabola since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(2)^{2} \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,4).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & ={x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(7)^{2} \\\\  & =49,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(7,49).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/4e/4e0ce399e32d7b957b71384d61cf08024b5d9995fde28d5f1fac71e1f123b2aa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "101089e6-8955-56e5-a9af-e1292704d66d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531964} {:answer/id #uuid "32a2455a-2737-50ee-86bb-360681e30611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532037} {:answer/id #uuid "85e130e6-d2bb-54dd-9b8e-4da5b5c088bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532238} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c11f76-19e6-5543-8fb4-9888b01581c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532307} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3a215d-79d6-530f-8102-b49ccd071024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532374}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532238} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d6ee34c-e06e-5482-9e88-a1329d3b48a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "69684600-8c5e-5874-b78e-2cbd727505f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-155534" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+x$ and the line $y=3x-1$ intersect at a single point with coordinates $(p,q).$ What is $p+q?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+x \\\\ y=3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x & =3x-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-3x+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=1.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. \nLet's choose the equation of the line. Substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-1 \\\\  & =3(1)-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the only point of intersection is $(1,2).$ Therefore, $p=1,\\,q=2,$ and \n\n$$\np+q=1+2=3.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/53/53b8622b0d2fa3fa756d5520d24849fb633539fb471037b9fce6c97464a5fc20.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532672 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a59d5def-358e-53b7-8955-ecdd2b78feb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532306}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532306} :answer-field/id #uuid "75cc18fd-6c53-54eb-b993-7a84046752bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532546} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b65d251-1335-5b26-a787-171e76d4951a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532057} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeeb0417-7377-5df8-aa31-302ace210d7c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "82d663f5-9675-5779-8562-0b4d4248c2b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-250076" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+2x-3$ and the line $y=5x+7$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ Find the values of ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}.$\n\n${y}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{y}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+2x-3 \\\\ y=5x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-3 & =5x+7 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x-10 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-2$ and ${x}_{2}=5.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =5{x}_{1}+7 \\\\  & =5(-2)+7 \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,-3).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =5{x}_{2}+7 \\\\  & =5(5)+7 \\\\  & =32,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(5,32).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/98/98d656438193d68da44573720da4a0fa5f7e1f7d253c29e0de2e309468c8b552.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532673 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84d4b28b-adba-57ec-9abd-1c6eab03e465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532232} :answer-field/id #uuid "21503cab-a324-5fe0-bffe-94e6d9d3497e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "82d7f69c-d6ad-553d-a4a8-4fc134f8a8d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335528" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.7),$ compute the value of $P(X>5)$ round to $4$ decimal places.\n\n$P(X>5)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.7),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(0.3)^{x-1}(0.7), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X>5.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\nP(X>5)=1-P(X\\le 5)\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4)+P(X=5) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{0}(0.7)+(0.3)^{1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{2}(0.7)+(0.3)^{3}(0.7)+(0.3)^{4}(0.7) \\\\  & =0.99757.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>5) & =1-P(X\\le 5) \\\\  & =1-0.99757 \\\\  & ≈0.0024.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532675 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ce64594-4d44-5fbb-916a-9540f6c9aad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1452" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532092} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc4bd4f-eaf1-5902-990b-f37f8dc13633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2186" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532211} {:answer/id #uuid "d55089b5-7956-5fa0-8147-9220f21301db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1630" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532391} {:answer/id #uuid "e07dadf3-01af-5381-bcd2-159701c2246f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1965" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532413} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c17dfa-72d1-519b-a04e-f7cb863e8d6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1406" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532415}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532415} :answer-field/id #uuid "5df74f69-e9ee-5e86-8090-3b562192d1f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "88f1dddc-e661-5d20-9bf7-86d209c9d4bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-97183" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right),$ compute $P(X\\ge 3)$ rounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{x-1}\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right), & x=1,2,3… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X\\ge 3.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{0}\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{1}\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right) \\\\  & ≈0.8594.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & ≈1-0.8594 \\\\  & =0.1406.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "904862d5-897c-534e-9e2f-e61c615561b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532262}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532262} :answer-field/id #uuid "abca62a1-f98f-5e60-8172-1b9e4959acc5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532571} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cef0f64-87f7-5719-93e3-ab9a559f0d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532093} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8833578-9795-544f-95d8-425c9b507a3a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8a3fea1f-f2c7-5a54-bfc4-1db59932f81e" :question/math-academy-id "q-250072" :question/problem "The parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+3x+15$ and the line $y=4x-5$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ Find the values of ${x}_{1}$ and  ${x}_{2}.$\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-{x}^{2}+3x+15 \\\\ y=4x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+3x+15 & =4x-5 \\\\ -{x}^{2}-x+20 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}+x-20) & =0 \\\\ -(x+5)(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-5,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-5$ and ${x}_{2}=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532682 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6884fc73-9e1c-5600-b1f9-aaba1f71b390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532166} {:answer/id #uuid "7f22556c-3b2e-524d-85bb-92b3a3ca508b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532220} {:answer/id #uuid "841b4151-23c6-5e4c-bcd4-1737cd56ac6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532231} {:answer/id #uuid "b208aace-0a45-5228-9c09-221962d12ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532327} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b32aba-933a-56f5-8b1d-d0dea1394bd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532422}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532220} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bbfb83c-6d7a-5008-8767-3335022b3066" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9485be5f-7e9e-52c2-8dc7-d3a495a119a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-152313" :question/problem "Given that the line $y=4x+k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y={x}^{2}+6x+7,$ what is the range of possible values of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=4x+k$ intersects the parabola $y={x}^{2}+6x+7,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+6x+7 \\\\ y=4x+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+7 & =4x+k \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+7-k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,\\,b=2,$ and $c=7-k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{{2}^{2}-4(1)(7-k)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{4-28+4k}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{-24+4k}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24+4k & <0 \\\\ 4k & <24 \\\\ k & <6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abedf875-4b66-5cf2-8796-0df622776e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532318} :answer-field/id #uuid "133c52bf-75f1-5b10-8319-1bbbc0724fa9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532501} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a82dbd44-f08e-5a33-b1b6-6f9773885612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532313} :answer-field/id #uuid "795ff076-e948-5018-81c9-39834569438a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b39dbd28-21a4-5509-83b2-cf250304af27" :question/math-academy-id "q-250085" :question/problem "The parabola $y=-{x}^{2}-4x$ and the line $y=4$ intersect at the point $P.$ Find the coordinates of $P.$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-{x}^{2}-4x \\\\ y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-4x & =4 \\\\ -{x}^{2}-4x-4 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}+4x+4) & =0 \\\\ -(x+2)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=-2.$\n\nWe know the y-coordinate of the intersection point from the equation of the line. So, the only point of intersection is $(-2,4).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/18/187c98be201ec084cdd4fb21f0347f9d768c251cfedcacc9532d754741199e14.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532707 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283ebc04-f8a2-5328-b526-0807928a5a14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532014} {:answer/id #uuid "d7787e12-314e-5739-b74c-e63de38ced70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532394} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a9def2-c17c-5098-82fa-fe65f39f5c2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532425} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ac17cb-7070-52ae-a020-36f3ef568515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532426} {:answer/id #uuid "f0dd39fb-017e-5809-bd44-92c3cd0e399c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532453}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532425} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c5c2381-aeb2-5e5f-8fa1-214d94fd8d00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d109f13f-6c22-5d50-a9f4-da3fbec07962" :question/math-academy-id "q-155523" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}-2$ and the line $y=x$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ What is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}-2 \\\\ y=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2 & =x \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+2=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532710 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aae80146-7711-5756-a3e1-b69c4a8b8bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532317}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532317} :answer-field/id #uuid "537c7c57-4db6-5792-bbce-5d1fd693dffa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532531} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec28ef51-9620-5155-9c04-545b698f6e12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532443}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532443} :answer-field/id #uuid "bef92fe3-e86f-53cd-bf4b-51bb44134db0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dace6513-0d3a-5c62-b857-525a505a8ea4" :question/math-academy-id "q-250156" :question/problem "Given that the line $y=3x+2$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+k,$ find the range of possible values of $k.$\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=3x+2$ intersects the parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+k,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+5x+k \\\\ y=3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+k & =3x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+(k-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=k-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{(2)^{2}-4(1)(k-2)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{4-4k+8}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{12-4k}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative. Setting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-4k & <0 \\\\ -4k & <-12 \\\\ k & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k>3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4be3563b-85a4-5927-8f14-75809dbb4706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3263" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532090} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9c899d-e556-5912-93c1-9e3ca0347975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3074" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532104} {:answer/id #uuid "551c66e8-4471-5f2d-9a69-c55890cd1f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3182" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532116} {:answer/id #uuid "68de0d90-b739-525e-a634-18b6e92914fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2901" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532167} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1a9056-6d3f-565a-ae29-98e576722262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3315" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532377}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532104} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd41d07a-8118-5a4f-af31-10c9c686bf80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e7dd6d27-99b8-51e0-8f07-28863587b350" :question/math-academy-id "q-97161" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.1),$ compute $P(4<X\\le 10)$ rounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.1),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.9)}^{x-1}(0.1), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4<X\\le 10) & =P(X\\in \\{5,6,7,8,9,10\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=5)+P(X=6)+P(X=7)+ \\\\  & \\,P(X=8)+P(X=9)+P(X=10) \\\\  & =f(5)+f(6)+f(7)+f(8)+f(9)+f(10) \\\\  & ={(0.9)}^{4}(0.1)+{(0.9)}^{5}(0.1)+{(0.9)}^{6}(0.1)+ \\\\  & \\,{(0.9)}^{7}(0.1)+{(0.9)}^{8}(0.1)+{(0.9)}^{9}(0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.3074.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e79b2f4c-88fd-5171-9805-d4c810b84f1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{124}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532434}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532434} :answer-field/id #uuid "635112d3-93b9-5181-b8a6-34ea068cbead" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ef957d04-4e42-5090-b9a6-8e161a911753" :question/math-academy-id "q-332586" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right),$ compute the **exact** value of $P(X\\le 3).$\n\n$P(X\\le 3)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x-1}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{0}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{1}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65ac0981-1b1d-5c23-9cc0-fc444715eee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533376} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9a387f-3c17-5e04-b07f-ec5133f1dfe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533601}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533376} :answer-field/id #uuid "40c5a737-0591-5fd5-8df7-bba8f7661150" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533833} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70dfdf1e-bde5-58a0-8f24-d392080fc911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533405} {:answer/id #uuid "a6dff475-5ce9-583a-91b1-fe2ebf698ee4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533405} :answer-field/id #uuid "563869eb-e9b6-592a-b3a5-fad42529fb9f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "130e77e3-e68d-5bfc-a672-f433d0d78798" :question/math-academy-id "q-333626" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has only one eigenvalue. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =4$ and it's positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f67469d62d6a0eb61397927924e55868437ad09f405c3b2a9d73567af00c4620.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "230537c8-69a6-557d-91bb-f030f6bb01c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ff/ff337e0120343c7c956afa5e49ea3b482fbd79fe363783a018842d1bfeb340d6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533224} {:answer/id #uuid "335d5523-771d-554d-9156-9e4fbbf3eece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/404c1f68f360e2916a6281033c8a7e9771b648c61156da903ce2f66b931b924e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533263} {:answer/id #uuid "67d912e0-e47b-5590-9c31-8a1b3ebc0a70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533383} {:answer/id #uuid "7110c95e-51b1-5868-be7a-c34f3b9a9768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/54/54eba0116ac434bdadbc878e8bd173b6174a42642cfcfe6825a8bdb178a3282c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533406} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4b1aa4-b78d-5e39-ae30-f82d43e13af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7e/7e48266d30ae7c204b25e6eddca968d1b4ce9cd8e1c7b19cd20247fa66070f0b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533679}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533224} :answer-field/id #uuid "f19bbfd2-a4f0-5fac-92d7-e5b144aac77f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6296be80-55c8-5a2e-8b17-a0c84de2ed61" :question/math-academy-id "q-333296" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-6t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/ff/ff337e0120343c7c956afa5e49ea3b482fbd79fe363783a018842d1bfeb340d6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d7b6960-ec55-5900-899c-bc269a25c30c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533399} {:answer/id #uuid "8787e948-643c-527a-bb41-b899213c3f81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533461} {:answer/id #uuid "8a308ba1-a690-5028-bd14-4b4c094a9be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533469} {:answer/id #uuid "c26e2d45-59a8-5ca1-907a-99bb13567a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533608} {:answer/id #uuid "f92eca85-a0a5-5dec-acb0-377d28dc68d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533750}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533399} :answer-field/id #uuid "efdeec04-664e-562e-b615-6cc0242e52ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b213b7e1-d060-5456-8c0f-13babec4bbc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-80502" :question/problem "![](images/15/155e2eac58e35942918ef52682ebfdad4df283409372ac4a54d20218c363704e.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-1,2]\\times [-1,2]$\n\nII. $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nIII. $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534071 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "277dde16-b64c-57ac-84b1-b5fcbd2bb63d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533237} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccb6452-a9e6-5252-8042-40dea1f911b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533396} {:answer/id #uuid "91580575-c469-5346-b827-2538c89c0475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533489} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e026ab-577b-5458-b42f-733bdaaa1c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533736}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533489} :answer-field/id #uuid "15641ed6-e248-5cf5-b89d-0f633c218ee7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533794} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a03247-cf62-59a7-95c3-d182171da9fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533187} {:answer/id #uuid "41aada98-8518-54a5-bd28-ef9faaa4d4df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533293} {:answer/id #uuid "68acd859-6c23-5e08-8a15-934efa318902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533385} {:answer/id #uuid "c46d755a-f8a5-58ac-b4ad-64046b2b8709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533187} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ece2493-8ef1-5e26-a1e2-c32f400a4304" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533802} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d746a04-d0b0-5b45-9d80-efba85b7276a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533398} {:answer/id #uuid "bd51ad30-641b-5ceb-aace-a3f015d67a3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533596}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533596} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b5f6e00-d58a-5b84-b573-730d3492f9a2" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :a